    "alert_id": "EX2_TDF_AKI_REVERSIBLE",
    "title": "AKI likely dehydration-induced; permanent TDF stop may be premature",
    "message": "Guidelines list TDF as a cause of acute kidney injury, Fanconi syndrome, and chronic kidney disease, and recommend RFT monitoring. The patient's creatinine rose sharply from 0.9 to 2.4 over ~12 months. However, the clinical picture strongly favours pre-renal AKI from severe gastroenteritis: 5 days of vomiting and diarrhoea, two household contacts with the same illness, hypotension (90/60), tachycardia, dry mucous membranes, reduced skin turgor, and a high urea-to-creatinine ratio of 24:1. Urinalysis shows no protein or glucose, arguing against TDF-induced tubular toxicity. Guidelines advise substituting the responsible ARV for severe toxicities. Given the pre-renal picture, TDF may be safely continued after rehydration and renal recovery rather than stopped permanently.",
    "issue_type": "plan_review",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Rehydrate and recheck creatinine in 48–72 hours. If creatinine trends toward baseline, TDF may be safely continued with ongoing renal monitoring rather than permanently discontinued.",
    "citations": [
      {"page_number": 37, "chunk_id": "hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__p37_c1"},
      {"page_number": 34, "chunk_id": "hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__p34_c2"}
    ]
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "EX2_TDF_RENAL_MONITORING_GAP",
    "title": "TDF renal monitoring overdue — 12-month creatinine gap",
    "message": "Guidelines require serum creatinine and eGFR monitoring for patients on TDF-containing regimens. The only prior creatinine was 375 days before today's visit (2025-02-14), and no creatinine was recorded at the 2025-08-20 routine visit despite the patient being on TLD for 2 years. This 12-month monitoring gap means early renal decline could have been missed. The current severe AKI (creatinine 2.4) might have been detected earlier with guideline-concordant monitoring. The clinician's plan does not specify a schedule for repeat creatinine after rehydration.",
    "issue_type": "monitoring_gap",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "After IV fluid resuscitation, recheck creatinine and eGFR within 48–72 hours, then establish regular TDF renal monitoring per guidelines.",
    "citations": [
